# Build V1.0 (internal: Build #38)

First stable, usable desktop on the RK3066 TV stick.
Date: 23.10.2026

**Legend:** ✅ observed on the device · ⚠️ not verified

## Summary

Mainline Linux 6.6.159 boots from the `recovery` partition. An Alpine Linux
rootfs image on the SD card provides Weston (Wayland) with the CPU pixman
renderer. Output is 1280x720 at 60 Hz, tear-free, with USB keyboard and mouse.

## Contents

| Component | Details |
|---|---|
| Kernel | Linux 6.6.159, dual core SMP, custom DT `rk3066a-hotack.dts` |
| Kernel image | `recovery-test.img` (~12.7 MB, ANDROID! format with loader SHA-1 patched) |
| Userspace | Alpine Linux v3.20 (armv7, musl), `rootfs.img` (1 GB) on the SD card (FAT32) |
| Compositor | Weston 12.0.4, DRM backend, pixman renderer, `xwayland=false` |
| Display | 1280x720@60 Hz, pixel clock 74.25 MHz, HDMI-A-1 |
| Input | Logitech USB receiver (keyboard + mouse) via libinput, `udevd` + `seatd` autostart |
| Panel | bottom, 24 h clock, launchers: terminal, wallpaper tool |
| Wallpaper tool | text menu in a terminal: 7 solid colors, default image, custom image from the SD card |
| Console | gadget serial on USB OTG (`/dev/ttyGS0`, appears as COM port on Windows) |
| Kernel cmdline | `consoleblank=0` |
| CPU clock | ⚠️ capped at 1.008 GHz in the DT; last session locked it to 816 MHz. Check the final value with `cpufreq` before release |

## Verified

- ✅ Tear-free output (Weston page flips on VBLANK)
- ✅ Smooth mouse and window movement (subjective, about 60 FPS)
- ✅ Keyboard and mouse work from boot without manual steps
- ✅ Stable at idle, no artifacts in the last session

## Known limitations

- No 3D/GPU acceleration. Lima probes, but GL clients caused artifacts.
- 1080p is unstable on the tested monitor (Samsung Odyssey G5), so 720p is forced.
- No Xwayland: X11 applications do not run.
- No browser or file manager. Dillo, Badwolf and PCManFM each triggered
  artifacts or freezes (cause not proven).
- The stick always boots Android first. Start Linux with `adb reboot recovery`.
- Needs stock Android for flashing and for copying `rootfs.img` to the SD card.
- No temperature monitoring or voltage scaling (DVFS), so the clock stays capped.

## How to run (tested on this device only)

1. Boot stock Android, enable USB debugging, connect ADB.
2. Copy `rootfs.img` to the SD card (FAT32), then `sync`.
3. Flash the kernel image to the character device in 16 KB chunks:
   ```sh
   dd if=recovery-test.img of=/dev/mtd/mtd3 bs=16384 && sync
   ```
4. Verify after `echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches` (see [timeline](../docs/timeline.md)).
5. `adb reboot recovery`
6. If the screen shows a FAT error, check the SD card (`fsck_msdos`).

## Files and checksums

| File | MD5 |
|---|---|
| `recovery-test.img` | fill in (`Get-FileHash -Algorithm MD5`) |
| `rootfs.img` | fill in |

## Recovery

The stock `kernel` and `boot` partitions are untouched. A power cycle boots
Android. The loader restores the stock recovery if the image check fails.
